{{/*
family: line-cli
what: flow
transports: v9, ipfix
reference: common line-oriented flow export commands (flow exporter, flow monitor); RFC 3954 (flow v9) and RFC 7011 (IPFIX)
verification: reference
*/}}
{{define "does"}}Exports the device's flow records to Woodshed as {{if eq .Transport "ipfix"}}IPFIX (RFC 7011){{else}}flow v9 (RFC 3954){{end}} over UDP.{{end}}

{{define "parts"}}
@@part cli - Enter in global configuration mode, then save the configuration
flow exporter WOODSHED
 destination {{.Address}}
 transport udp {{.Port}}
 export-protocol {{if eq .Transport "ipfix"}}ipfix{{else}}v9{{end}}
{{- if .SourceInterface}}
 source {{.SourceInterface}}{{end}}
 template data timeout 60
flow monitor WOODSHED-MONITOR
 exporter WOODSHED
 record <FLOW-RECORD>
interface <MONITORED-INTERFACE>
 ip flow monitor WOODSHED-MONITOR input
{{end}}

{{define "notes"}}
Flow export commands differ more than any other here: some devices use one `ip flow-export destination {{.Address}} {{.Port}}` line with a version keyword, some an exporter and monitor pair as above, some a policy that matches traffic first. Treat this as the list of values to set.
The export version keyword differs too: use your device's keyword for {{if eq .Transport "ipfix"}}IPFIX{{else}}version 9{{end}}.
<FLOW-RECORD> is a flow record the device defines (many have a built-in one with addresses, ports, protocol and counters); <MONITORED-INTERFACE> is the interface whose traffic to export.
The template timeout (60 s) makes the device resend its templates every minute, so Woodshed can decode its flows soon after a restart.
{{end}}

{{define "check"}}Woodshed's Flows view lists the device as an exporter within a minute or two, with its templates and sampling rate.{{end}}
